WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.600
Cloud detection often begins with signals from identity systems, audit logs,

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:10.180
control-plane activity, network telemetry, storage events, or cloud security tools.

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:16.400
Suspicious login activity, unusual API calls, privilege changes, new access keys,

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:21.580
exposed storage, abnormal data transfer, unexpected compute creation,

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:24.840
and configuration changes can all require triage.

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:29.940
The first job is to understand what the alert says and what evidence supports it.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:33.440
Triage should separate confirmed evidence from hypotheses.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:37.500
A responder may know that an access key was created at an unusual time.

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:42.760
It may be only a hypothesis that the key was used maliciously until API logs,

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:47.620
identity context, source information, and related activity are reviewed.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:51.920
This discipline matters because premature conclusions can lead to the wrong containment

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:54.220
action or misleading communication.

00:00:54.440 --> 00:01:00.600
Severity assessment depends on scope, privilege, data sensitivity, exposed services,

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:03.540
active misuse, and business impact.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:08.560
A suspicious read-only event in a test account is different from privilege escalation in a

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:10.780
production tenant with sensitive data.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:16.200
Responders should identify affected accounts, regions, identities, resources,

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:20.580
storage locations, workloads, and data sets as early as possible.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:22.960
Initial analysis should build a timeline.

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:24.620
What happened first?

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:26.760
Which identity performed the action?

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:27.940
From where?

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:30.000
What permissions were available?

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:31.900
What resources were touched?

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:33.720
Were logs changed?

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:35.940
Were keys created or used?

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:39.440
Was data accessed, modified, or transferred?

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:44.060
Timeline thinking helps responders connect related events without overfitting on one

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:45.000
alert.

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:49.140
Triage is not the same as final attribution or root cause analysis.

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:53.520
Early work is about reducing uncertainty enough to make safe response decisions.

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:59.100
Document what is known, what is suspected, what evidence supports each point,

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:01.480
and what follow-up is required.

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:04.580
This keeps the response grounded when pressure rises.
